INT. SCHOOL BUS - FOG ROAD - NIGHT

A yellow bus idles on a country road, headlights swallowed by white fog. The windows are fogged on the inside too. Rows of green vinyl seats, empty except for four.

MABEL TRASK, fifties, driver, grips the big wheel in cracked leather gloves. She wears a cardigan and a hairnet and has not spoken since the engine stalled. Behind her, in the first seat, CORA, ten, hugs a lunchbox. Across the aisle, her brother BAXTER, eight, draws a face in the window glass. In the rear, hunched against the emergency door, OSWIN, fourteen, wears headphones around his neck.

BAXTER
Why'd we stop?

MABEL
Fog.

CORA
That's not a reason.

MABEL
It's the only one I've got.

OSWIN
(from the back)
There's something in the road.

CORA ^
There's something in the road.

Mabel leans forward. The headlights show only white. Then, at the edge of the beam, a tall figure, motionless, in a long coat, head tilted.

MABEL
Stay in your seats.

BAXTER
Is it a man?

MABEL
I don't know.

The figure takes a step. Then another. Mabel's hand slides to the door lever. The knuckles whiten.

OSWIN
Don't open it.

MABEL
I'm not going to.

The figure lifts a hand. Taps the glass of the windshield, once, with one long finger.

Mabel shuts off the headlights. Dark. Four sets of breathing.
